\begin{lemma}[Invertible Differential Gives a Local Diffeomorphism]
\label{lem:local-diffeomorphism-from-invertible-differential}

\uses{lem:smooth-local-inverse-on-open, lem:invertible-operators-open}




Let $M$ and $N$ be smooth manifolds, let $F : M \to N$ be smooth, and let $p$ be an interior point of $M$ at which $dF_p$ is invertible. Then $F$ is a local diffeomorphism at $p$: there are open sets $U \ni p$ and $V \ni F(p)$ such that $F|_U : U \to V$ is a diffeomorphism.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

\uses{lem:smooth-local-inverse-on-open,lem:invertible-operators-open}
Let $\varphi$ and $\psi$ be the charts of $M$ and $N$ around $p$ and $F(p)$, and let $g = \psi \circ F \circ \varphi^{-1}$ be the coordinate representation of $F$, defined and smooth on the open set $\Omega$ of chart points whose image under $F$ lies in the domain of $\psi$. Because $p$ is an interior point, the chart image of a neighborhood of $p$ is a neighborhood of $a = \varphi(p)$ in the model space, so $dg_a$ is the ordinary differential of $g$ at $a$ and it is invertible, being $dF_p$ read in the charts.

Since $g$ is $C^\infty$ on $\Omega$, its differential $x \mapsto dg_x$ is continuous there, and by Lemma \ref{lem:invertible-operators-open} the set of $x \in \Omega$ at which $dg_x$ is invertible is open. Shrinking, choose an open $s \ni a$ contained in $\Omega$ on which $dg_x$ is invertible for every $x \in s$. The inverse function theorem gives a local homeomorphism $R$ with $R = g$ near $a$; restrict it to $s$. Its inverse is $C^\infty$ on the open set $R(s)$ by Lemma \ref{lem:smooth-local-inverse-on-open}, so $R$ restricted to $s$ is a diffeomorphism of $s$ onto the open set $R(s)$.

Conjugating back, $U = \varphi^{-1}(s)$ and $V = \psi^{-1}(R(s))$ are open, contain $p$ and $F(p)$ respectively, and $F|_U = \psi^{-1} \circ R \circ \varphi$ is a composite of diffeomorphisms, hence a diffeomorphism onto $V$.
\end{proof}

The most useful consequence of the inverse function theorem is the following. A smooth map $F: M \to N$ is said to have \textbf{constant rank} if the linear map $dF_p$ has the same rank at every point $p \in M$.

